[{:seon.task/name "program-cost-follows-what-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by lane LOOP (2026-10-21) once main holds Seon's own source (seon.source, 54 files, 1,168 forms, 2,278 program keys): seon.merge/program reads every live form and definition of a db, 256 ms per call on that program (measured in the test runtime, load 8), and three-way calls it three times per merge, per :branch-diff and in judge-tx (reconcile.clj, `mp (pm/program main #{})` and the branch's), so every attempt pays ~1 s of whole-program reads whatever it changed (bin/check loop: an 11 s run for one merge). Cause: merge.clj `program` (live-forms + def-entity per key) is O(program), not O(change). Correct: the branch's changes since its merge base come from Datahike's history between the base and the head (the datoms the branch's transactions touched), and only those keys are read on base, ours and theirs; main's changes since the base the same way. Replaces the whole-program reads in three-way's callers (writer branch-merge, reconcile judge-tx, the dashboard's diff); keeps `program` for tests and the census. Lane INPLACE (2026-10-21, BUILD plan P4): with merges loading in place and no build, this is what is left of the loop's turn: `seon.e2e/loop-turn!` takes 13.4 s from release to quiet on 1,641 forms (the implement attempt 4.8 s, the merge attempt 6.9 s, write-back commit and quiescence 1.6 s), against the plan's target of under 1 s; seon.merge/program measured 443 ms per call and seon.program/live-forms 368 ms on that program in a release root (load 7). The loop's turn under a second needs this task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-cost-test/a-three-way-costs-the-changes-not-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "conflict-value-states-its-input"
  :seon.task/plan "bin/check census (census-2) fails on `seon.merge/conflict-value` (src/seon/merge.clj): its contract is `[:=> [:cat :any] :seon.merge/conflict]`, so census generates nil or 0 as input and the answer cannot be a conflict map (found by lane shrink-dead, 2026-10-22; main's binary fails the same way). Its real input is a merge entry: a Datahike entity (merge.clj, `entries`) or a pulled map (problems.clj, `(:seon.attempt/conflicts p)`; task.clj) holding :seon.merge/key and :seon.merge/kind. A schema resource cannot hold an inline fn (Malli's interpreter is not in the build: `:malli.core/sci-not-available`), and an entity is not a map, so a plain `:map` input would refuse the entity caller. Correct: a registered schema for a merge entry that validates by its members and generates as a pulled map, registered where seon.contract registers host schemas (as :seon.host/db and others are, with their generators), and conflict-value's contract names it; or callers pass `(d/pull ...)` maps and the input is a plain `:map` of the :seon.merge/* members. Proof: census green on this function, and the three callers' tests stay green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-conflict-value-reads-a-pulled-entry}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-uncontracted"
  :seon.task/plan "Worktree session DUP-ROOT, audit row A4, verified on main c3265a8. Which of a branch's functions lack a contract is computed three ways from three sources: `seon.writer` merge-refusal over the three-way merge's `:ours` (the branch's changes since its merge base, and only while `contract/on?`), `seon.reconcile` judge-tx over `pm/program` of the branch against main's (every fn entry that differs from main, whether or not the branch changed it, and with contracts off too), and `seon.harvest/uncontracted` over the live runtime's vars (now `seon.contract/uncontracted`, dup-helpers). So the writer's merge gate and root's judge disagree: a branch behind a main that added a contract to f still holds the old uncontracted f, which the judge refuses as the branch's gap and the writer admits; with contracts off the judge refuses what the writer merges. The cause: no owner of the question over a program value. Correct: `seon.merge/uncontracted` (ours: a three-way's `:ours`) answers the sorted fn symbols without a contract, empty while contracts are off; the writer's gate calls it; the writer's `:branch-diff` answer (one derivation, seon.merge/three-way) carries `:uncontracted` and `:unmerged`, and judge-tx reads them from the :diffed answer instead of building `pm/program` twice and `pm/unmerged` again. The agent's own done check (`seon.turn`) asks the same function of its branch's changes since its merge base (each form it evaluates is recorded on its branch as it runs), so `seon.harvest/uncontracted` and the gaps it held at the attempt's start go. Deletes about 10 lines and two whole-program builds per judged attempt."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/uncontracted-is-what-the-branch-changed seon.reconcile-test/the-judge-and-the-merge-gate-agree-on-contract-gaps}}
]
